// #2523 / #2612: a MARKER `Eq` -- one declared with no methods -- cannot make a
// non-scalar compare by content, so the BOUND is refused at that instantiation
// rather than answering by reference identity.
//
// This file used to rely on the PRELUDE's `Eq` being a marker. It is not one
// any more: ADR-0097 gave it `equals(Self, Self) -> Bool`, so a `[T: Eq]` bound
// at a `derive (Eq)` struct dispatches through a real witness and compares by
// content (`fixtures/eq_bound_derive_test.vibe`). The guard did not go with it
// -- a PROGRAM may still declare its own marker `Eq`, and for that one the
// reasoning is unchanged, so the trait is declared here.
//
// Measured before the guard, on the production linear/RC lane: `bare(p1, p2)`
// on two EQUAL, distinct `Pt` answered `false` and `bare(p1, p1)` on the same
// object answered `true`, while `p1 == p2` at a concrete site answered `true`.
//
// The BOUND is refused at this instantiation, not the impl -- `impl Eq for Pt`
// stays a legal declaration (a marker impl can be a tag), and a concrete
// `p1 == p2` still compares structurally
// (fixtures/derive_struct_eq_marker_impl_test.vibe).

trait Eq

impl Eq for Int

struct Pt {
  v: Int
}

impl Eq for Pt

fn bare[T: Eq](x: T, y: T) -> Bool {
  x == y
}

fn main() -> Unit allows Console {
  println(__to_string(bare(Pt::{ v: 1 }, Pt::{ v: 1 })))
}
